{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"a97a2f54155c440a","name":"card_unit_meteor","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/OceaniaStorm/units/card_unit_meteor","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"METEOR","text":"Remove after this unit attacks. Add a copy to your deck with double attack and defense.","flavorText":"The Gloster Meteor was Britain’s first operational jet fighter and the only Allied jet aircraft to fly combat missions in WWII. It was built by the Gloster Aircraft Company around turbojet work pioneered by Frank Whittle and Power Jets. The Meteor first flew in 1943 and entered RAF service with No. 616 Squadron in July 1944. A total of 3,947 Meteors were built. Early Meteors were kept under strict secrecy and were first used over Britain against V-1 flying bombs. On 4 August 1944, Meteor pilots claimed their first V1s. The RAF did not initially allow the aircraft to fly over German-held territory, to prevent a crashed jet from being captured. In 1945, Meteor F.3s joined the Second Tactical Air Force in Belgium and later moved into Germany. They flew armed reconnaissance and ground attack missions, but never fought German jets in the air. The Meteor was not as fast or heavily armed as the German Me 262, yet it gave the RAF valuable experience with jet aircraft. It also became an important postwar fighter and test aircraft.","faction":"Britain","type":"fighter","rarity":"Unique","cost":4,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":1},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_meteor","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_meteor_title","SourceString":"METEOR","LocalizedString":"METEOR"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":1,"defense":1,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_meteor_text","SourceString":"Remove after this unit attacks. Add a copy to your deck with double attack and defense.","LocalizedString":"Remove after this unit attacks. Add a copy to your deck with double attack and defense."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_meteor_history","SourceString":"The Gloster Meteor was Britain’s first operational jet fighter and the only Allied jet aircraft to fly combat missions in WWII. It was built by the Gloster Aircraft Company around turbojet work pioneered by Frank Whittle and Power Jets. The Meteor first flew in 1943 and entered RAF service with No. 616 Squadron in July 1944. A total of 3,947 Meteors were built. Early Meteors were kept under strict secrecy and were first used over Britain against V-1 flying bombs. On 4 August 1944, Meteor pilots claimed their first V1s. The RAF did not initially allow the aircraft to fly over German-held territory, to prevent a crashed jet from being captured. In 1945, Meteor F.3s joined the Second Tactical Air Force in Belgium and later moved into Germany. They flew armed reconnaissance and ground attack missions, but never fought German jets in the air. The Meteor was not as fast or heavily armed as the German Me 262, yet it gave the RAF valuable experience with jet aircraft. It also became an important postwar fighter and test aircraft.","LocalizedString":"The Gloster Meteor was Britain’s first operational jet fighter and the only Allied jet aircraft to fly combat missions in WWII. It was built by the Gloster Aircraft Company around turbojet work pioneered by Frank Whittle and Power Jets. The Meteor first flew in 1943 and entered RAF service with No. 616 Squadron in July 1944. A total of 3,947 Meteors were built. Early Meteors were kept under strict secrecy and were first used over Britain against V-1 flying bombs. On 4 August 1944, Meteor pilots claimed their first V1s. The RAF did not initially allow the aircraft to fly over German-held territory, to prevent a crashed jet from being captured. In 1945, Meteor F.3s joined the Second Tactical Air Force in Belgium and later moved into Germany. They flew armed reconnaissance and ground attack missions, but never fought German jets in the air. The Meteor was not as fast or heavily armed as the German Me 262, yet it gave the RAF valuable experience with jet aircraft. It also became an important postwar fighter and test aircraft."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","hasBlitz":true,"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/METEOR.METEOR","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_meteor_photo","SourceString":"A RAF Gloster Meteor F3 jet fighter in Melsbroek, Belgium, 1945.","LocalizedString":"A RAF Gloster Meteor F3 jet fighter in Melsbroek, Belgium, 1945."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftM'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Medium/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftM.193"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeMedium_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeMedium.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackFighter.BP_AttackFighter_C","SubPathString":""},"threat_level":0.3},"variables":[{"name":"attTotal","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"defTotal","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_meteor","owner":"card_unit_meteor_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_ReturnValue","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_Event_defenderCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_Event_wasShockAttack","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"K2Node_Event_attackCost","type":"int32",